WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:04.890
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.290
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.870
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.130
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.270
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.770
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

7
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.970
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

8
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.010
We're The Office Lady 6.0.

9
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.480
Oh, hi there.

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.260
We have a really special episode today.

11
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.020
We're so excited about it.

12
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:38.460
We are going to be revisiting Boys and Girls episode from Season 2 with a very special gueSt.

13
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:39.480
That's right.

14
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.000
This is Season 2, Episode 15.

15
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.500
Boys and Girls is very special to us for a number of reasons.

16
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.980
First, because this episode was based on an idea that we pitched to Greg on set one day.

17
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:52.560
That's right.

18
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:57.100
We were standing, I remember, by the copier that was between reception and accounting.

19
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.280
And we were chit-chatting.

20
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.100
And we were like, Greg, Greg, we have an idea for an episode.

21
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.940
And he walked over, and we both said, look, we temped in corporate America,

22
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.920
and we would have to go to these, like, women seminars.

23
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.860
Like, women-in-the-workplace seminars.

24
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.260
And so we said, wouldn't it be funny if Jan came in and threw a women-in-the-workplace seminar,

25
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.780
and Michael got all jealous, and he threw a men-in-the-workplace seminar.

26
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.100
And Greg was like, oh, my gosh, I love it.

27
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:24.620
Wait, wait.

28
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:25.960
BJ, come here.

29
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.340
Yeah, BJ was sitting over by Creed.

30
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.360
And he was like, get over here.

31
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.340
Grab a notepad.

32
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.200
And literally, he was like, jot this down.

33
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.040
And he was like, tell him what you just told me.

34
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.000
So we told BJ, same thing.

35
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.000
And then Greg goes, then what happens?

36
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.980
And we were like, no idea.

37
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:42.600
We don't know.

38
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.280
That's all we got.

39
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.760
But BJ was like, okay, okay.

40
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.800
And he would ask us some questions.

41
00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:53.140
And I think there were, like, a number of things that kind of made their way into the episode

42
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.800
that were based on some of the questions that BJ asked us.

43
00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.620
And then he got assigned to write it.

44
00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.780
Boys and Girls.

45
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.260
Yes.

46
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.180
So fun.

47
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.220
Well, it was also really special to us because it was directed by Denny Gordon,

48
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:12.820
who at the time in 2006 was only the second woman to direct an episode of The Office.

49
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.500
And she joined us in the studio to talk about her time on the show.

50
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.300
This was such a wonderful interview.

51
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.480
Oh, my goodness.

52
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.560
From the minute she walked in the door, you'll hear.

53
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:24.380
Yes.

54
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:25.360
And you'll get it.

55
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.440
She's just amazing.

56
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.260
She just walked in the door and hugged us and told us how proud she was of us.

57
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:30.640
I know.

58
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.180
And we were, like, trying not to tear up because we respect her so much.

59
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:34.580
Yeah.

60
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.000
We actually wrote about Denny Gordon in our book, The Office BFFs.

61
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:47.400
We did a little research for the book, and we found that of the 55 different directors that we had on The Office,

62
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.360
only six were women.

63
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.320
Denny directed two episodes of The Office, actually.

64
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.760
She directed The Secret and Boys and Girls.

65
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.680
She is such a trailblazer among female directors in film and television.

66
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:07.000
She was one of the first women to graduate from the Yale School of Drama with an MFA in directing.

67
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:13.340
She has directed over 100 hours of network television as well as films and commercials.

68
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.640
She has a very long and impressive resume.

69
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.880
And she's also directed several episodes of John Krasinski's show Jack Ryan.

70
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.280
Yeah, and we talked to her about that, too.

71
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:23.840
We did.

72
00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.440
We also shared a photo in our book that we took during the filming of Boys and Girls.

73
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.740
It's really special to us.

74
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.620
We were basically on set one day, and we looked around and realized it was just us gals that were working the whole day.

75
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.560
So we all gathered around Denny for a picture.

76
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:41.520
It's so great.

77
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.020
You have to share it in stories, Ange.

78
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:43.200
I will.

79
00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.340
We're also going to discuss the extra footage added to the superfan version of Boys and Girls.

80
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:57.520
So the original broadcast ran at 21 minutes and 21 seconds, and the extended cut has an additional 10 minutes and 84 seconds.

81
00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.640
So that's a lot of extra scenes.

82
00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:06.500
Well, when we talked to Dave Rogers about creating the superfan episodes, he said Boys and Girls was one of his favorites.

83
00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.440
He gave us his list of top 10 superfan episodes, and this is one of them.

84
00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.380
I loved watching it.

85
00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.520
I mean, I can see why he said that.

86
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:16.800
Yeah.

87
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.960
So many of the scenes are extended.

88
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.120
And also, not only are they extended, but they really add to the story.

89
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.960
Yes, exactly.

90
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.360
I think really enrich the story.

91
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:28.980
That's right.

92
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.860
Well, before we go to break, let me give a quick summary of the episode.

93
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:39.460
But rather than have me summarize the episode, let's hear what Pam has to say.

94
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.320
Today's a women in the workplace thing.

95
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:51.440
Jan's coming in from corporate to talk to all the women about, um, I don't really know what, but Michael's not allowed in.

96
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.380
She said that about five times.

97
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.720
And then, of course, Michael has his version of what's happening.

98
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:01.660
Let's hear that.

99
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:08.580
Yeah, translation, I have been banned from my own conference room so that Jan can talk in secret to all the girls.

100
00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.900
Oh, sorry, women of the workplace.

101
00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:11.560
About what?

102
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:11.960
I don't know.

103
00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:12.900
Clothes.

104
00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:13.820
Me.

105
00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.500
He's so put out that they're talking about me.

106
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:20.600
I know.

107
00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:21.440
Probably him.

108
00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:21.780
Yeah.

109
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.000
Well, Michael gets upset when he's excluded from the seminar.

110
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.360
So, of course, he's going to have his own meeting with all of the men down in the warehouse.

111
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:38.540
And meanwhile, in this episode, this is the one where Jan tells Pam about a special design program that the company is offering.

112
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.020
Jim is encouraging her to do it.

113
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.600
Roy is not.

114
00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.840
And ultimately, Pam decides not to pursue it.

115
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.160
Very dramatic arc in this one.

116
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.000
That Pam talking head kind of breaks my heart.

117
00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:51.680
I know.

118
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.500
Well, the Superfan episode starts with a whole new cold open.

119
00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.020
And, lady, I loved it because it's more of Michael versus technology.

120
00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.600
Yes, it's very funny.

121
00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.200
Well, it starts with Michael hanging out at front reception.

122
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:13.620
Yeah, he tells Pam that he's really overwhelmed because he just spent $400 on a new phone because he liked the ringtone, only to learn that you can choose any ringtone you want.

123
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.220
He thought the ringtone came specifically with the phone.

124
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.620
But also, lady, he has a flip phone.

125
00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.080
He spent $400 on a flip phone?

126
00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:24.760
I don't know.

127
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.360
Did they ever cost that?

128
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.640
I wanted to look it up, but I didn't.

129
00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.420
And then Michael's, like, going through all the different ringtones.

130
00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:39.100
Some are, like, classical, but the one that he really loves makes the sound kind of like a jackhammer, and he makes the noise.

131
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.180
It's like, grr, grr, grr, grr.

132
00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.560
And Jim goes, do you mean vibrate?

133
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.360
Because they all have vibrate.

134
00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.660
They all have that.

135
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.600
A little bit of old tech made me chuckle.

136
00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.540
Well, listen, why don't we go to break, and when we come back,

137
00:06:53.640 --> 00:07:00.320
you are going to hear our conversation about this Peacock extended episode of Boys and Girls with director Denny Gordon.

138
00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:01.800
And it is not to be missed.

139
00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.030
Attention all new and existing members of the WDNC.

140
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:14.850
I'm Melanie Sanders, and this is the We Do Not Care Club podcaSt.

141
00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:19.110
We got to midlife and realized we just simply do not care much anymore.

142
00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:27.210
Now we got to help each other make sense of hot flashes, itchy ears, chin hair, trifling families, brain fog, and bat wings.

143
00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:30.070
We are not alone, and we will be heard.

144
00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.970
So come join the WDNC.

145
00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:37.510
Follow and listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcasts.

146
00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:44.910
And welcome to the studio.

147
00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.610
I am super excited to be here.

148
00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.270
I'm fans of your show, and thank you for having me.

149
00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.690
I mean, it took me back to look at all the old episodes, and what a blaSt.

150
00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.030
How deliciously fun.

151
00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:01.190
Normally, I can't go back and look at episodes, and this was just beyond fun.

152
00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.010
So thank you.

153
00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:02.910
Oh, wow.

154
00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.930
Well, we have been wanting to talk to you for a long time.

155
00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:09.970
So you really just made our dreams come true by being here today.

156
00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:11.190
Bless you.

157
00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:11.670
Bless you.

158
00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.270
Now, you directed two episodes of The Office.

159
00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.730
You directed The Secret and Boys and Girls.

160
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.250
We always ask people this question.

161
00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.810
How did you get your job directing The Office?

162
00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:27.830
Well, I had done a couple of shows with our beloved Ken Guapas, and we knew each other.

163
00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.030
And I think he started talking to Greg Daniels about me.

164
00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:33.890
And I think Greg and I had the same agent.

165
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.210
So I think Richard White's super agent was, you know, blah-blahing about me.

166
00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:39.950
So I got an interview.

167
00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.450
And, of course, you were the hottest show on the planet at the time.

168
00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.250
I mean, and continued to be so, like, I was so thrilled.

169
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:51.150
I thought, even if I don't get a job, I'm just so excited to be in the mix and have a chance to talk.

170
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.550
And I met Greg at the Earth Cafe.

171
00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.990
And what was crazy was it was not even an interview.

172
00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:03.350
It was like Greg just started downloading to me all these wonderful idiosyncrasies about the show.

173
00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:09.930
You know, how you shot it and your process and how you created this environment and how special it was.

174
00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:16.190
And just all these tips on how I might, you know, come live in that world with you for a while.

175
00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.590
So when I got a chance to come over, I thought I'd died and gone to heaven.

176
00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:20.570
Wow!

177
00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:32.050
And they said that a lot of times conversation went to all kinds of places and lasted like an hour.

178
00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:35.350
And it sounds like you guys had a great first meeting.

179
00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:36.670
It was a great first meeting.

180
00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.150
He dropped so much knowledge on me.

181
00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:42.770
So many things I still remember to this day and think of in all the work that I'm doing.

182
00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:50.350
One of the things he said was how important it was to just remember we were a documentary.

183
00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:57.390
And it had never dawned on me, even though I'd been devoted to your show and watching, was, you know, that sometimes, you know,

184
00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.370
we would rehearse something and then there'd be a swish pan to rain or, you know, something.

185
00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.370
And how that always had to stay spontaneous.

186
00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:11.970
And how you had to keep the camera late in getting there because you don't know that magical moment is going to happen.

187
00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:16.150
And so it, you know, really required a very lively camera.

188
00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:17.710
And of course, Randall was awesome.

189
00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:19.750
And the operating was so key.

190
00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.550
And it was just so of the moment.

191
00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:30.070
And when Greg started talking about that and how we keep that so that it feels like we're capturing life, which was so critical on this show.

192
00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:34.030
When he started talking about that, it was just so amazing.

193
00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.590
And yeah, I think our conversation spun around a lot of ways.

194
00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:46.290
But he did really get me so excited and intrigued because I began to see how you worked and the process.

195
00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:50.550
You know, there's this secret little world and we closed the door and all the crew is gone.

196
00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:52.610
And there we are.

197
00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.950
And you're powdering your own nose and you're journaling at your desk.

198
00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:01.130
And everybody is just, you know, you remember everybody's just behaving.

199
00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:04.690
And it was such a magical time and place.

200
00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.590
I've never experienced that anywhere before.

201
00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.130
So it was such a gift.

202
00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:10.810
Oh, my God.

203
00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.070
How did you prep for directing the show?

204
00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:19.370
Well, crazily enough, I had been making a film in the UK.

205
00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.730
So I had watched the UK version.

206
00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:22.910
I was out of my mind over.

207
00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.650
And I remember saying to somebody, a producer, we should buy the rice.

208
00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:28.510
Of course, you guys, I think we're already in production.

209
00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:31.550
But I watched, of course, all of season one.

210
00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:35.190
And I think it was such a well-oiled machine, you guys.

211
00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:38.350
I mean, I think I had the script well before I came.

212
00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.650
And I think I came over a couple of days to observe.

213
00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:41.190
I don't know.

214
00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:45.590
Maybe Ken was directing and I got a chance to observe because it was so unusual.

215
00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:47.170
It was pretty rare air.

216
00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:53.090
And how you guys worked and how you achieved what you were achieving really needed to be a good fit.

217
00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:59.110
And so I was super keen to fit in and fall in line.

218
00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:04.510
Of course, Ken Coop is the hardest act to follow on the planet as a director.

219
00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:06.210
It's like, oh, my God, we're not worthy.

220
00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:14.750
And I remember thinking, with this group of superstars, I don't need to be doing your homework for you.

221
00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.610
You will do your homework for you.

222
00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:26.510
You know these people so well that, man, I'm just lucky if I can come in and maybe figure out what's the most fun place to play this scene.

223
00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:28.850
Where's the chance for physical comedy?

224
00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:30.210
That's how I can help.

225
00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:40.130
But that you guys knew yourself so well that it was just, how do I keep that atmosphere going so you can live and breathe in this moment?

226
00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:40.810
Yeah.

227
00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.890
You certainly put the camera, I thought, in some really fantastic places.

228
00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:49.430
And if you watch the Superfan episodes, there are a few tags and things.

229
00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.910
There's a shot of Madge in the warehouse pushing her cart.

230
00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.710
And the camera is really low on the ground.

231
00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:59.350
So you're kind of looking up her neck and up her nose a little bit.

232
00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.310
And she's just pissed off at just being a part of it.

233
00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:06.030
But it just built that moment so wonderfully.

234
00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:07.190
Oh, thank you.

235
00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:07.490
I know.

236
00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.370
I'd forgotten about so much of that.

237
00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.790
That's what was so delicious about these extended episodes.

238
00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.010
These Superfan episodes are crazy.

239
00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:14.430
Great.

240
00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:15.310
Aren't they amazing?

241
00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:15.810
Yes.

242
00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:16.410
Yes.

243
00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:22.610
It's so, there was, especially this episode, there was so much extra.

244
00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:24.610
It was able to breathe.

245
00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:32.010
In fact, let's jump in because this episode opens and Jan has like gathered all the women in the conference room.

246
00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.850
She's going to start her Women in the Workplace seminar.

247
00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:38.770
But Michael, of course, is super jealous and annoyed that he's not included.

248
00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:40.690
So he keeps busting in.

249
00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:42.350
Jan eventually throws him out.

250
00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:48.850
The Superfan episode is so great because it just includes more stuff from Jan.

251
00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:56.370
She talks about how men who are assertive are respected, but women who are assertive get called the B word or the C word.

252
00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:01.510
And it just kind of builds even more on the premise, which I really loved.

253
00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:06.390
And we were curious, what were your thoughts when you first read this script?

254
00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.970
Like, did this speak to you in any way?

255
00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:10.610
It definitely spoke to me.

256
00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.770
It totally spoke to me.

257
00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:16.490
You know, at this point in my career, I think I had been everywhere I'd gone to direct.

258
00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.750
I was usually the only woman in the whole season.

259
00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:22.090
So it's like I really, you know, was relating.

260
00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.770
It's so much better now and we've still got miles to go.

261
00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.990
But at the time, I was always like, you know, the lonely chick.

262
00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:33.090
And I remember thinking how great the script was that B.J.

263
00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:44.450
Novak wrote and that we were going to get to see all of you guys reacting to this idea, you know, pre-human resources, pre-canceling, like women in the workplace, boys and girls,

264
00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.450
you know, what do people really think of these issues?

265
00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:55.610
And it was, I just thought, if I do it right, we're going to have like all these wonderful surprises about your characters and how you really feel about things.

266
00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:57.070
It was a goldmine.

267
00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:57.930
And I knew, you know, B.J.

268
00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:02.890
had just like, it was the tip of the iceberg and there was always going to be so much extra.

269
00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:06.530
I mean, I remember we were 10 minutes over in the extra super version.

270
00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.270
We had 10 minutes of gold that the audience didn't get to see.

271
00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:09.590
Yeah.

272
00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:13.690
And so glad for that because jokes didn't even have time to pay off.

273
00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:14.010
Yeah.

274
00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:15.530
You know, in lots of ways.

275
00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:20.050
Like the fact that the guys had unloaded, that Michael had told him to unload the truck.

276
00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:21.810
And we thought, okay, well, that goes.

277
00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.950
But then we realized that that was a shipment going out.

278
00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:25.630
Yeah.

279
00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:25.940
Yeah.

280
00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:29.950
So, you know, we really needed that time to let it breathe.

281
00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.230
So, yeah, thank God for the super episodes.

282
00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:39.270
I wanted to talk about a scene that's extended in the super fan that I thought really built with that extra footage.

283
00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:44.890
So, you know, Michael has come into the conference room and he's interrupted this women's meeting.

284
00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:47.910
And Jan throws him out and he just can't take it.

285
00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:50.090
He can't take it that the ladies have this meeting.

286
00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.190
So, he gathers the guys in the bullpen.

287
00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:55.510
Of course, he sits right up against that conference room window.

288
00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:56.890
He even kind of bangs it.

289
00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.710
And they're so loud.

290
00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:07.750
And the way you cut the scene back and forth in the extended version, you can actually hear how inappropriate Michael's being.

291
00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:13.950
Because you actually go into the conference room, whereas in the original, the way it's edited, they're outside the whole time.

292
00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:16.070
And I thought, Denny, we should hear it.

293
00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:16.770
Yes.

294
00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:17.450
Oh, God.

295
00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:18.050
What fun.

296
00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:18.650
My favorite.

297
00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:20.950
Yeah, he's really banging against that glass.

298
00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:21.250
Yeah.

299
00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:26.630
So, to be in a position of leadership, you often need to be assertive, right?

300
00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.190
You have to be able to state a firm opinion.

301
00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:32.750
And this means that you have to give up the fantasy of being everyone's friend.

302
00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.950
Well, first of all, I just want to warm up a little bit.

303
00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:44.730
Now, I know this is going to sound crazy to you, but in India, they do this thing called laughter therapy.

304
00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:46.170
And I want to try it out.

305
00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:50.390
It's when a group of people just get together and they laugh as hard as they can.

306
00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:52.410
So, I want to try that with you right now.

307
00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:55.130
On the count of three, let's laugh as hard and loudly as we can.

308
00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:55.790
Ready?

309
00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:57.150
One, two, three.

310
00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:07.880
Oh, my goodness.

311
00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:09.120
Let's just clap.

312
00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:09.800
Let's just clap.

313
00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:10.140
Ready?

314
00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:12.260
Yeah.

315
00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:13.580
Yeah.

316
00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:14.880
That's what I'm talking about.

317
00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.460
I don't know what you're doing here, Michael.

318
00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:19.120
Just having a little guys in the workplace thing.

319
00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:21.460
Oh, my God.

320
00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:22.500
I love that so much.

321
00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:27.240
And what was so great, I think we discovered this in rehearsal, was, you know, what's Jan going to do?

322
00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:32.420
And she goes back in and closes the blind so that they're not even there.

323
00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:35.560
There was another physical comedy moment, I think we discovered.

324
00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:38.060
But, yeah, that teed up everything that's going to follow.

325
00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:38.840
That's right.

326
00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.300
And I loved it so much.

327
00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:42.120
God, there was just so much there.

328
00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.120
And what a poignant episode.

329
00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:48.800
I remember, you know, and going around the room and hearing from everybody on your side.

330
00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:52.140
And every, you know, you all had such interesting reactions.

331
00:17:52.520 --> 00:17:56.080
I think you said you were in the most mature relationship of your adult life.

332
00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:56.480
Yes.

333
00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.080
And then we cut to you and Rain being super awkward going through a door.

334
00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:00.620
I know.

335
00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:06.080
You know, and then the reveal of your interest in art and that you actually started a beautiful drawing.

336
00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:06.660
Yes.

337
00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:07.120
Yes.

338
00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:08.080
Tell me about that.

339
00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.340
Did you just make that up on, was that in the script or did you just do that?

340
00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:22.800
That was Phil Shea, the prop master, gave me that notebook with that sketch in various forms of being done because I couldn't draw something like that.

341
00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:30.100
And so that was like his contribution that I be like kind of doing that sketch.

342
00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.280
And I know I noticed that, too.

343
00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.140
I thought, what a great detail, right?

344
00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.020
Yeah, such a fantastic detail.

345
00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:40.320
And, you know, the thing that is so incredible, you know, in our life,

346
00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:54.160
we're maybe blessed to be on a show where everyone cares so much about what we're doing that our prop man would be so deeply involved in the story that he would think that for you and offer that up to you.

347
00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.920
I mean, that was part of the magic of the thing.

348
00:18:56.060 --> 00:19:01.340
Every single person in that family was rooting for the best show possible.

349
00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:02.740
I mean, that's just one example.

350
00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:03.220
Yeah.

351
00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:04.800
That's truly how it felt, though.

352
00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:09.560
Like, we're only as good as our weakest person or, you know, like everyone was a collective.

353
00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:15.080
And, Denny, you just came right in to the fold just like that.

354
00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:17.140
Like, I felt like you elevated us.

355
00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:19.580
And Jenna and I have talked about this on the podcaSt.

356
00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:24.100
Just we had this moment where we all looked around and we're like, it's only us gals.

357
00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.220
There's only gals on the set today.

358
00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:26.820
Yes.

359
00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.800
And how special that was in that moment.

360
00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:30.020
It was.

361
00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:30.540
It was.

362
00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:40.220
And I just, I thought it was just so wonderful, you know, that sitting in the, you know, in the conference room and being able to, you know, get reactions from each of you.

363
00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.940
And, of course, each woman coming from her different place and time.

364
00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:45.920
You remember Kate's line.

365
00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.060
She says, hi, I'm Kate.

366
00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:47.880
And I'm an out.

367
00:19:49.840 --> 00:20:01.580
It was just so full of gold and everybody having, you know, Mindy wanting an SUV and what everybody wanted was so in character and such a reveal.

368
00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:05.980
I think the audience, I don't know, did the audience love the episode as much as we three do?

369
00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:07.680
I think they do.

370
00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:14.760
And people remind me a lot of Pam's dream to have a terrace that she can plant flowers on.

371
00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.300
I get asked that question a lot.

372
00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:21.080
Do I think Pam ever got her terrace with flowers?

373
00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:26.200
Because that, and in the Superfan episode, that's even more extended.

374
00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:29.640
Like her talking about that and what that means to her.

375
00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:32.000
I really loved that.

376
00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:36.860
That was based on a story that I told BJ Novak about reading a book when I was younger.

377
00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.620
It was like a choose-your-own-adventure book.

378
00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:44.000
And the girl in the book had a house with like a, what do you call that?

379
00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:48.880
It almost looks like a little tower, you know, where it's like a round room, kind of a.

380
00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:50.020
Like a Rapunzel tower.

381
00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:51.720
A little bit like a Rapunzel tower.

382
00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:57.680
And the girl in the book would just, this was her special place and she got a new little sister,

383
00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:00.420
but she could go to her tower and daydream.

384
00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:06.540
And I used to think, oh, I hope one day I can have a house with like my own tower room.

385
00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:10.760
And I'll just make it my own little library where I read books and be creative.

386
00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.040
It just sounded like so wonderful.

387
00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:17.860
And he turned that into a terrace with plants.

388
00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:23.560
So I think when I was doing those talking heads and as Pam, when I was talking about them,

389
00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:28.420
I was trying to like summon the little girl in me who wanted her tower house.

390
00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:29.600
Beautiful, beautiful.

391
00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:34.300
And I think that's why it's so relatable too, because you, every little kid does have a dream

392
00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:39.660
like that, you know, of something that feels a little magical, right?

393
00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:40.860
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

394
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:45.900
I love that it resonates with the audience, you know, and that you told the story so well.

395
00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:50.180
You know, it's not a lot to ask that you can have a room with some plants and a view,

396
00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.460
you know, and it's such a lovely dream.

397
00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:59.440
I think that really kicked off the whole poignancy of your dreams and then the whole art school

398
00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:05.620
subplot or the art training subplot, which was so great in the script and the way you played it,

399
00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:10.720
that we know this is something that your hearts desire, but like you're already,

400
00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:15.080
even though Jan's trying to make it possible for you, you're already thinking of all the reasons

401
00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:15.840
why not?

402
00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:16.280
Yeah.

403
00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:24.380
And how much it meant that, you know, Roy versus Jim, that, you know, Roy was never going

404
00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.340
to be on board with this, but who's your greatest supporter?

405
00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.780
Who's your biggest fan is Jim?

406
00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:35.480
And the whole poignancy of that arc and how sometimes we all give up on our dreams at some

407
00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:37.340
point because it's just too hard.

408
00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:42.020
Or because you don't have the cheerleader in the place where you need it the moSt.

409
00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:47.840
I mean, if the person you're living with and spending your life with isn't lifting up

410
00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:52.800
your dreams and aspirations, that is a really hard wind to push againSt.

411
00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:58.700
No, and I think that we began, you know, we, the audience, we saw that at the, and it relates

412
00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:04.780
to the episode that I did prior to this in The Secret when you find out that that crush that

413
00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:07.820
everybody's talking about Jim having is not in the paSt.

414
00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:13.800
It's right here, right now and how you are just, and you played it so beautifully, just

415
00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:17.280
like so secretly thrilled that it's today.

416
00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:21.260
And then, and then you're going down in the elevator together and then we in the, we, the

417
00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:24.640
audience are going, keep them together, keep them together, please, please, please.

418
00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:27.080
We need this love to, to blossom.

419
00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.740
It just teed up everything so well that followed.

420
00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:30.160
Yeah.

